  - Adds or reuses a standard `UAIPerceptionComponent` on an AIController Blueprint and persists standard `UAISenseConfig_Sight` settings without serializing UnrealMCP references.
- `inspect_ai_behavior_assets(behavior_tree_path="/Game/Cubeless/AI/Smoke/BT_AIDummyPatrolChase.BT_AIDummyPatrolChase", blackboard_path="/Game/Cubeless/AI/Smoke/BB_AIDummyPatrolChase.BB_AIDummyPatrolChase")`
  - Reloads saved BT/BB assets through the C++ bridge helper and reports root shape, child tasks, Blackboard keys, chase decorator/abort state, editor graph presence, and MCP dependency audit status.
- `run_ai_behavior_tree_execution_smoke(pawn_asset_path="/Game/Cubeless/Reactive/BP_Piper_ReactivePrototype", skeletal_mesh_path="/Game/Cubeless/Character/Piper/SK_Piper", anim_blueprint_path="/Game/Cubeless/Character/Animation/ABP_RPGChracter", label_prefix="MCP_AIBTExecutionSmoke", report_dir="Docs/Analysis/AIDummySmoke", behavior_tree_path="/Game/Cubeless/AI/Smoke/BT_AIDummyPatrolChase.BT_AIDummyPatrolChase", blackboard_path="/Game/Cubeless/AI/Smoke/BB_AIDummyPatrolChase.BB_AIDummyPatrolChase")`
  - Starts SIE, loads the saved BT/BB assets, calls `AIController.use_blackboard` and `AIController.run_behavior_tree`, writes the patrol/target Blackboard values, verifies movement evidence, and cleans up temporary actors.
- `remove_niagara_modules(system_path, module_names=None, module_name_prefixes=None, module_name_contains=None, module_name="", emitter_index=None, emitter_name="", allow_source_edit=false, save=true, request_compile=true, wait_for_completion=false, timeout_seconds=10.0, poll_interval_seconds=0.1)`
  - Native editor-only Niagara stack cleanup command. Reconnects the parameter-map chain and removes matched module input nodes when cleaning stale modules from authored systems.
- `cleanup_niagara_renderers(system_path, remove_renderer_indices=None, clear_meshes_binding_renderer_indices=None, expected_renderers=None, expected_renderer_count=None, emitter_index=None, emitter_name="", dry_run=true, ...)`
  - Native dry-run-first renderer cleanup. Exact expected renderer class/enabled contracts guard disabled-renderer deletion; binding resets additionally require the current `meshes_binding_parameter`, and requested compile waits must complete before saving.
- `set_niagara_module_static_switch(system_path, input_name="", value=None, ..., expected_current_value="", wait_for_completion=true, require_compile_gate_for_save=true)`
  - Native static-switch discovery and guarded mutation. Enum display/internal/numeric inputs are canonicalized through the pin's actual `UEnum`; hidden/MAX values and stale current-value contracts are rejected, and the safe Python default requires an actual compile, stable post-request IDs, final pin readback, and affected-script cached/computed ID synchronization before saving.

## Design Notes

`pcg_tools.py` writes Unreal Python results to a JSON file in the temp directory because Unreal's Python command result capture is limited for multi-line scripts. The MCP tool reads that JSON file and returns structured data.

UE 5.6.1/5.7 multi-line scripts that include `import` statements should run through `ExecuteFile`. `ExecuteStatement` works for simple one-line commands but fails for the PCG helper's generated scripts.

The PCG component operations are native-first where a focused C++ bridge exists. `refresh_pcg_components` sends cleanup/refresh/generate requests through the C++ bridge and returns component state/readback without sleeping on the editor game thread. When `wait_until_complete=true`, the MCP Python tool polls the native command externally with `generate=false` until PCG busy state clears or the timeout expires. The Unreal Python fallback remains best-effort and does not block for completion because blocking Python can stall editor ticking. `set_spline_component_points` uses the native helper to avoid transient `TRASH_` spline components when available.

PCG attribute/property selector structs are native-only. Use `set_pcg_attribute_selector` for settings properties such as `WeightAttribute`, `MatchAttribute`, `InputAttribute`, `InputWeightAttribute`, and `SetTarget`. Do not mutate these selectors through `execute_python`, `PCGAttributePropertySelectorBlueprintHelpers.set_*`, or Python wrapper methods like `set_attribute_name`; that path has triggered `PyWrapperStruct.cpp` ensures and editor shutdown access violations. The native command only edits ordinary PCG settings properties on the target graph and does not create UnrealMCP nodes or saved `/Script/UnrealMCP` dependencies.

Persisted-reference mutation APIs must reject MCP-only class, object, enum, function-owner, component, actor, material expression, and PCG settings references. The guard blocks `/Script/UnrealMCP`, `/UnrealMCP`, `UnrealMCP`, `MCPUnreal`, and `mcp_unreal` strings before authored assets can store those paths. This keeps UnrealMCP as an editor bridge rather than a required runtime/content dependency unless a user explicitly accepts that coupling.

Editor level transitions should use `open_editor_level` instead of Python
`load_level`/`load_map` calls. The command defaults to `dry_run=true` and
returns target existence, current world, dirty package blockers, and whether a
real load would be scheduled. Real loads are deferred to a later editor tick and
must be confirmed by polling `get_editor_level_open_status` with the returned
`request_id`. Real loads are blocked by default when dirty packages exist,
avoiding the previous Python-frame world-reference crash path and avoiding
silent loss of unsaved editor state.

New temporary preview maps should use `safe_new_preview_map` instead of Python
`new_blank_map` or `new_map_from_template` calls. The command defaults to
`dry_run=true`, requires targets under `/Game/_MCP_Temp` unless explicitly
overridden, blocks dirty-package creation by default, and creates/saves the map
through the native C++ bridge.

The AI texture tools now use a Codex built-in image-generation handoff rather
than API-key image calls. Once a BaseColor PNG exists,
`generate_pbr_companion_textures` can create first-pass heuristic Normal,
Roughness, AO, and Metallic PNGs; `import_texture_set_to_unreal` imports those
files as Texture2D assets; `create_material_with_texture_set` or
`import_create_and_apply_texture_set` creates a PBR material/material instance
and can apply it to a selected/named mesh target.

Scripted source imports use one bridge-owned structured policy for both ticker dispatch and Interchange scope. The default `interchange_policy="auto"` intentionally does not inspect Python source and resolves conservatively to `legacy`, so helper aliases, `getattr`, and persistent globals cannot make an import silently lose its bypass. `legacy` supports synchronous `AssetImportTask`/`import_asset_tasks` and `AutomatedAssetImportData`/`import_assets_automated`: immediately before Python execution C++ captures value and priority for `Interchange.FeatureFlags.Import.Enable`, `Interchange.FeatureFlags.Import.FBX`, `Interchange.FeatureFlags.Import.FBX.ToLevel`, and `Interchange.FeatureFlags.Import.OBJ`; it disables and verifies them, then restores them in reverse order and verifies the original state. Known texture-format Interchange CVars are tracked so older nested console guards cannot leave stale priority history. `interchange_policy="preserve"` and `interchange_policy="interchange"` keep Interchange enabled for synchronous direct `UInterchangeManager.import_asset`, `reimport_asset`, or `import_scene` calls, including Interchange-dependent formats such as OpenVDB/Sparse Volume Texture and USD. Start-only `scripted_*_async` calls must wait for all tasks and perform readback before Python returns; otherwise the bridge reports `interchange_async_outcome_unknown` with `do_not_retry=true`. `interchange_policy="none"` is a trusted assertion that the script cannot import and skips the import ticker/CVar scope. Every import-capable policy requires a standalone next-tick dispatch and is rejected inside synchronous `batch` or re-entrant execution. A legacy callback scheduled after `ExecPythonCommandEx` returns is outside the bypass scope and is unsupported.

The AI smoke tools use `AIController` movement and line-of-sight checks in SIE
with temporary actors in the current level. They do not save level content or
create runtime project code. UE 5.6 exposes Behavior Tree and Blackboard
factories to Unreal Python, but not every stock Behavior Tree node class, so
BT/BB asset authoring now uses the narrow editor-only C++ helper instead of
mutating Behavior Tree graph internals from Python. Generated assets stay on
standard `AIModule` classes and are audited for MCP-only references before
success. The saved BT execution smoke uses stock `BTTask_MoveDirectlyToward`
for no-NavMesh temporary levels; a production NavMesh-backed patrol tree can
switch to `BTTask_MoveTo` later without changing the dependency model.

SkyDomeTools also includes a polar UV cloud layout pass. The flow starts from a supplied polar SkyDome UV texture, extracts modular cloud card textures, places editor-only card actors for layout validation, captures a viewport screenshot, and blocks Hunyuan mesh generation until that card layout is accepted. The polar Hunyuan batch bridge then submits validated modules to Hunyuan with texture generation forced off and records generated mesh paths back into the polar manifest. Hunyuan should be used for detailed geometry only, not texture generation, in this pipeline.

## Update Guidance

When the user asks for "최신 업데이트", "업데이트 풀", "원본 pull", "upstream 최신", or similar wording, the expected behavior is:

- Keep the PCG/Python extension.
- Pull/fetch from `origin`.
- Fetch from `upstream`.
- Fast-forward local `main` from `origin/main`.
- Start a no-commit merge from `upstream/main`.
- Resolve conflicts in favor of preserving:
  - generic `execute_python` C++ bridge
  - `Python/tools/python_tools.py`
  - `Python/tools/pcg_tools.py`
  - AI texture services/tools/docs
  - tool registration in `Python/unreal_mcp_server.py`
  - docs and update script
- Run Python verification before committing the merge.
- Commit the pending merge only after verification passes.
- Push `main` to `origin` only when push was requested.

Use this shape when updating:

```powershell
.\scripts\update_with_pcg_extension.ps1
```

If the remote names are not set yet, the recommended layout is:

- `upstream`: `https://github.com/chongdashu/unreal-mcp`
- `origin`: `https://github.com/cubeless2-lgtm/UnrealMCP5_6_1.git`

